---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { plugins } from "../data/plugins";
import RequestFlow from "../components/RequestFlow.astro";
const featuredPlugins = ["usage_logger", "tool_governor", "keyword_compactor"]
  .map(name => plugins.find(plugin => plugin.name === name)!);
---

<Layout>
  <section class="frame hero">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal style="--i:0">Local-first · open source · WASM-extensible</p>
      <h1 class="display" data-reveal style="--i:1">A programmable proxy for coding agents<span>.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede" data-reveal style="--i:2">Keep your agent and your model. See request activity locally, apply your own traffic policy, and extend it with Go or Rust plugins. Torana runs between your coding agent and its configured provider.</p>
      <div class="actions" data-reveal style="--i:3">
        <a class="button primary" href="/quickstart/">Try Torana locally <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="button" href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge">Explore the source ↗</a>
      </div>
      <p class="muted">Runs on your machine. No cloud deployment or Torana account required.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/docs/support/">Supported harnesses →</a>
    </div>
    <RequestFlow />
  </section>

  <section class="rule-section compact">
    <div class="frame">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <h2 class="display">Your workflow, beyond one harness.</h2>
          <p>Keep the reusable parts of your setup in Torana. Try an existing plugin, or write the small piece you keep rebuilding.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="capability-grid">
        <article><h3>Keep your agent and model.</h3><p>Add Torana between a supported coding harness and its provider. Your harness still owns tool execution and its approvals.</p><a class="text-link" href="/docs/support/">Check compatibility →</a></article>
        <article><h3>Write the logic once.</h3><p>Work with a shared request format across supported OpenAI, Anthropic, and Gemini APIs. Reuse plugin logic across compatible harnesses without parsing each provider’s JSON yourself.</p><a class="text-link" href="/docs/support/#shared-format">How formats work →</a></article>
        <article><h3>Go or Rust. Compiled to WASM.</h3><p>Build small plugins with the provided SDKs, without modifying Torana itself. Start with an example and make it fit your workflow.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-plugin-sdk">Explore the SDKs ↗</a></article>
        <article><h3>Choose what plugins can do.</h3><p>Inspect requested permissions, approve access, and control the plugin pipeline running on your machine. Installation alone does not grant access or enable a plugin.</p><a class="text-link" href="/docs/plugin-installation/">Review the lifecycle →</a></article>
        <article class="community-capability"><div><h3>Install a plugin. Share yours.</h3><p>Try a plugin from someone else’s repository, or keep your own workflow hacks in yours. Install from source with the CLI, then review and approve the built plugin. No central publishing step required.</p></div><div class="community-links"><a class="text-link" href="/plugins/">Find a plugin →</a><a class="text-link" href="/plugins/submit/">Share your plugin →</a></div></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rule-section compact">
    <div class="frame">
      <div class="catalog-head">
        <div><h2>A few plugins to start with.</h2><p class="muted">Three examples. Pick what helps your workflow.</p></div>
        <a class="button" href="/plugins/">Browse all {plugins.length} plugins →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="catalog catalog-preview">
        {featuredPlugins.map((plugin) => (
          <article class="plugin">
            <h3><a href={`/plugins/#${plugin.name}`}>{plugin.name}</a></h3>
            <p>{plugin.description}</p>
            <a class="tag" href={`/plugins/#${plugin.name}`}>Details →</a>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rule-section quick">
    <div class="frame quick-grid">
      <div>
        <h2 class="display">One binary. One local port.</h2>
        <p>You need Git, Go 1.26.6 or newer, and a provider credential. Start with no plugins, then add the usage logger. The walkthrough includes how to switch back.</p>
        <a class="button" href="/quickstart/">Open the walkthrough →</a>
      </div>
      <p class="quick-outcome">Send one request through the proxy. See it in the local feed. Then add a plugin.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rule-section">
    <div class="frame story">
      <h2 class="display">This was supposed to be a compactor.</h2>
      <p>The idea: compact large tool and MCP outputs with a cheaper or local model before they pile up in a coding agent’s conversation history.</p>
      <p>In typical side-project fashion, the reverse proxy built for the experiment got over-engineered into a WASM plugin runtime. The compactor didn’t establish reliable savings in the tested setup. The proxy became the project.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/blog/why-torana/">How Torana got here →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="rule-section compact">
    <div class="frame">
      <div class="section-head"><h2 class="display">Make it part of your workflow.</h2><p>Try Torana on a coding task. Hack together a plugin for your own use case—a context policy, a tool integration, or that bit of glue you keep rebuilding. Share what you make.</p></div>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/quickstart/">Try your first session →</a><a class="button" href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/issues">Share feedback ↗</a><a class="button" href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md">Contribute ↗</a></div>
      <p class="muted">Torana runs locally; your requests still reach the provider you choose. <a class="text-link" href="/docs/support/">Check compatibility</a> before trying your agent.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Hallmark · Workbench / Ecosystem Index · five-panel platform refinement
   * pre-emit critique: P5 H4 E4 S5 R5 V4 · existing theme, nav and footer retained
   * rendered: mobile/desktop and light/dark pass · text contrast >= 6.79:1
   */
  .capability-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-4); }
  .capability-grid article { border: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: var(--space-5); display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; }
  .capability-grid h3 { font-size: 1.25rem; line-height: 1.25; margin: 0; }
  .capability-grid p { color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 60ch; }
  .capability-grid a { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; white-space: nowrap; margin-top: auto; }
  .community-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .catalog-head h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .catalog-head p { margin: 0; }
  .quick-outcome { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.25rem); line-height: 1.3; margin: 0; max-width: 24ch; }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .capability-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .capability-grid .community-capability { grid-column: 1 / -1; flex-direction: row; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--space-6); background: var(--paper-2); }
    .community-links { flex-shrink: 0; flex-direction: column; }
  }
</style>
